3
00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:16.679
Unit fifteen: Biography & Memoir. Today you will express regret and wishes with wish and if only.

4
00:00:16.979 --> 00:00:19.550
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:23.675
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.517 --> 00:00:25.912
Life narrative.

7
00:00:26.917 --> 00:00:30.902
Life narrative means the story of a person's life told as a connected account.

8
00:00:31.517 --> 00:00:34.073
The life narrative begins in a small village.

9
00:00:34.683 --> 00:00:37.591
Your turn. Say: Life narrative.

10
00:00:40.433 --> 00:00:41.609
Life narrative.

11
00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:43.792
Personal reflection.

12
00:00:44.783 --> 00:00:48.873
Personal reflection means thoughtful consideration of your own experience.

13
00:00:49.483 --> 00:00:51.854
Personal reflection gives a memoir depth.

14
00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:55.509
Your turn. Say: Personal reflection.

15
00:00:58.367 --> 00:00:59.774
Personal reflection.

16
00:01:00.517 --> 00:01:02.342
Chronological structure.

17
00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:07.625
Chronological structure means arranging events in the order in which they happened.

18
00:01:08.217 --> 00:01:10.530
The book has a chronological structure.

19
00:01:11.117 --> 00:01:14.721
Your turn. Say: Chronological structure.

20
00:01:17.567 --> 00:01:19.171
Chronological structure.

21
00:01:19.917 --> 00:01:21.545
Formative experience.

22
00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:26.686
Formative experience means an event that strongly shaped a person's character.

23
00:01:27.283 --> 00:01:29.422
A flood was her formative experience.

24
00:01:30.017 --> 00:01:33.261
Your turn. Say: Formative experience.

25
00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:37.798
Formative experience.

26
00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:40.039
Voice and perspective.

27
00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:45.534
Voice and perspective means the writer's personal style and way of seeing events.

28
00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:48.684
Voice and perspective make each memoir unique.

29
00:01:49.283 --> 00:01:53.201
Your turn. Say: Voice and perspective.

30
00:01:56.067 --> 00:01:57.579
Voice and perspective.

31
00:01:58.317 --> 00:01:59.991
Historical context.

32
00:02:00.983 --> 00:02:05.537
Historical context means the events and conditions of the time in which a life was lived.

33
00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:08.893
Historical context explains his choices.

34
00:02:09.483 --> 00:02:12.658
Your turn. Say: Historical context.

35
00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:17.476
Historical context.

36
00:02:18.217 --> 00:02:19.427
Memoir writing.

37
00:02:20.417 --> 00:02:23.566
Memoir writing means writing about memories from your own life.

38
00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:26.626
Memoir writing needs honesty and detail.

39
00:02:27.217 --> 00:02:29.927
Your turn. Say: Memoir writing.

40
00:02:32.767 --> 00:02:34.046
Memoir writing.

41
00:02:34.783 --> 00:02:35.959
Authenticity.

42
00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:40.587
Authenticity means being true to real experience and feeling.

43
00:02:41.183 --> 00:02:43.566
Authenticity is what readers value most.

44
00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:47.290
Your turn. Say: Authenticity.

45
00:02:50.133 --> 00:02:51.343
Authenticity.

46
00:02:52.133 --> 00:02:55.981
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:56.817 --> 00:03:01.907
Visal, a memoirist writes 'I wish I had listened to my grandmother.' What does wish do here?

48
00:03:03.017 --> 00:03:15.729
It expresses regret about the past. Wish + past perfect: I wish I had listened. If only is stronger: If only I had asked her more questions. Both refer to things that did not happen.

49
00:03:16.817 --> 00:03:18.131
And for the present?

50
00:03:19.217 --> 00:03:31.378
Wish + past simple for a present situation: I wish I knew her recipe. And wish + would for annoyance or hope about someone else: I wish people would listen.

51
00:03:32.483 --> 00:03:33.810
A common mistake?

52
00:03:34.917 --> 00:03:48.752
Using would have after wish: not 'I wish I would have gone'. Say 'I wish I had gone'. Wish and if only both move one tense back from the reality they describe.

53
00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:51.633
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:52.483 --> 00:03:57.629
Grammar focus: Regrets and wishes: wish and if only plus past perfect.

55
00:03:57.929 --> 00:03:59.116
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:59.817 --> 00:04:01.863
I wish I had listened to my grandmother.

57
00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:05.260
If only she had told me her story earlier.

58
00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:07.999
I wish I knew her recipe.

59
00:04:08.933 --> 00:04:20.229
Wish / if only + past perfect = regret about the past. Wish + past simple = present wish. Wish + would = wish for change in someone else. Never use would have after wish.

60
00:04:20.983 --> 00:04:24.251
Be careful! Don't say: I wish I would have gone.

61
00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:28.327
Say: I wish I had gone.

62
00:04:28.627 --> 00:04:31.103
Use past perfect after wish for the past.

63
00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:34.483
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:35.267 --> 00:04:36.337
Looking Back.

65
00:04:36.637 --> 00:04:41.493
A memoir is a life narrative written from the writer's own voice and perspective.

66
00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:47.913
Personal reflection often begins with a formative experience that the writer still remembers clearly.

67
00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:54.104
Many memoirists admit that they wish they had understood the historical context of their parents' lives.

68
00:04:55.100 --> 00:05:02.115
A chronological structure helps readers follow the story, although honest writers know that memory is not always orderly.

69
00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:07.422
Authenticity matters most, so a writer should tell the truth even if it is uncomfortable.

70
00:05:08.533 --> 00:05:12.465
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:13.317 --> 00:05:15.537
I wish I blank harder at school.

72
00:05:15.837 --> 00:05:18.991
A, had worked. B, would work. C, have worked.

73
00:05:22.533 --> 00:05:24.626
The answer is A: had worked.

74
00:05:25.783 --> 00:05:29.629
The writer's personal style and way of seeing events is voice and blank.

75
00:05:29.929 --> 00:05:33.884
A, perspective. B, structure. C, citation.

76
00:05:37.433 --> 00:05:39.503
The answer is A: perspective.

77
00:05:40.650 --> 00:05:42.406
Which sentence is correct?

78
00:05:42.706 --> 00:05:48.414
A, If only I would have asked her.. B, If only I had asked her.. C, If only I have asked her..

79
00:05:51.967 --> 00:05:54.930
The answer is B: If only I had asked her..

80
00:05:56.083 --> 00:06:00.184
An event that strongly shaped a person's character is a blank experience.

81
00:06:00.484 --> 00:06:04.509
A, chronological. B, formative. C, personal.

82
00:06:08.067 --> 00:06:10.635
The answer is B: formative.

83
00:06:11.783 --> 00:06:14.491
According to the reading, why should a writer tell the truth?

84
00:06:14.791 --> 00:06:20.256
A, authenticity matters most. B, to make the book longer. C, to avoid dates.

85
00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:26.763
The answer is A: authenticity matters most.

86
00:06:27.967 --> 00:06:31.443
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:32.283 --> 00:06:37.127
What makes a memoir feel 'authentic' to readers, even though it is one person's perspective?

88
00:06:45.917 --> 00:06:47.487
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:47.787 --> 00:07:11.723
A memoir feels authentic when the writer is honest about feelings, mistakes and uncertainty. Specific details, a natural voice and personal reflection show that the experience is real. Readers know it is one perspective, but they trust a writer who does not pretend to be perfect and who admits what they wish they had done differently.

90
00:07:12.023 --> 00:07:13.790
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:14.683 --> 00:07:21.271
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and regrets and wishes: wish and if only plus past perfect.

92
00:07:21.571 --> 00:07:26.755
Today's value is authenticity. Lead as your true self, guided by your real values.

93
00:07:27.055 --> 00:07:29.974
See you in Unit sixteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:30.274 --> 00:07:34.169
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

